data.content:
Your code **doesn't use `Thread.Abort`**, so there's no direct migration issue here. However, the pattern of manually spawning threads is outdated for .NET 8. The modern equivalent:

```csharp
public class SimpleWorker
{
    public async Task RunBatchAsync(List<WorkItem> items)
    {
        await Parallel.ForEachAsync(items, async (item, ct) =>
        {
            await Task.Run(() => Process(item, ct), ct);
        });
    }

    private void Process(WorkItem item, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        item.Result = ComputeResult(item.Input);
    }
}
```

**Key migration points:**

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` + cooperative cancellation |
| Manual `new Thread(...)` | `Task.Run` / `Parallel.ForEachAsync` |
| `Thread.Join()` | `await` / `Task.WhenAll` |

**Why `Thread.Abort` is gone:** It threw `ThreadAbortException` at arbitrary points, risking corrupted state. .NET 8 throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` if called. Replace with `CancellationToken` — pass it through your call chain and check `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at safe points.
